WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:09.399 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the South America capitals, episode one of three. First, I clear
the chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:18.754 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We begin in the north: the Andean capitals of Venezuela, Colombia and Ecuador,
and the two Guianas on the Atlantic coast.

3
00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:21.377 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Caracas into view.

4
00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:36.002 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Caracas stands in a valley of the northern Andes, a dozen kilometres inland
from the Caribbean, with the wall of El Ávila rising between the city and the
sea.

5
00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:43.862 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for the Caracas people who lived in the valley, it was the birthplace of
Simón Bolívar in 1783.

6
00:00:44.514 --> 00:00:52.060 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Caracas as the city of Bolívar, tucked under the wall of Ávila
between the mountains and the Caribbean.

7
00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:54.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Georgetown into view.

8
00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:08.166 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Georgetown stands on the Atlantic coast of Guyana, at the mouth of the
Demerara river, a little north of the Essequibo.

9
00:01:08.826 --> 00:01:21.794 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for King George III, its wooden St George's Cathedral is one of the
tallest wooden churches in the world, and much of the city lies below sea
level behind a sea wall of the 19th century.

10
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:29.950 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Georgetown as the wooden cathedral capital, guarded by the sea wall
that holds back the Atlantic.

11
00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:33.319 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Paramaribo into view.

12
00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:45.874 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Paramaribo lies on the Suriname river, about fifteen kilometres from the
Atlantic, on the north coast of Suriname.

13
00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:56.293 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its inner city of wooden Dutch colonial houses is a UNESCO site, and a mosque
and a synagogue stand side by side on the same street.

14
00:01:56.951 --> 00:02:03.174 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Paramaribo as the Dutch wooden town where a mosque and a synagogue
share one street.

15
00:02:03.863 --> 00:02:06.371 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bogotá into view.

16
00:02:10.513 --> 00:02:20.579 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bogotá lies high in the eastern Andes, on a plateau about 2,640 metres above
the sea, in the centre of Colombia.

17
00:02:21.232 --> 00:02:38.764 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It grew from Bacatá, the Muisca capital, meaning "enclosure outside the
farmland"; the Spanish added Santa Fe, and the two names competed for
centuries. Its Gold Museum holds some 55,000 pieces of pre-Hispanic goldwork.

18
00:02:39.428 --> 00:02:46.162 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Bogotá as the high plateau capital where the Muisca Bacatá and the
Spanish Santa Fe still share the name.

19
00:02:46.846 --> 00:02:49.354 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Quito into view.

20
00:02:53.923 --> 00:03:03.559 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Quito stands in the Andes of Ecuador at about 2,850 metres above the sea, a
capital city on the equator.

21
00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:15.736 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its old centre was among the first places added to UNESCO's World Heritage
list, in 1978, and it lies below the Pichincha volcano, which last erupted in
1999.

22
00:03:16.388 --> 00:03:26.582 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Quito as the capital of the equator, one of the first on UNESCO's
list, sitting under a volcano that erupted in 1999.

23
00:03:37.715 --> 00:03:51.833 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Caracas sits under the Ávila wall by the Caribbean, Bogotá and Quito climb
high into the Andes, and Georgetown and Paramaribo are the two small colonial
ports of the Guianas.

24
00:03:52.483 --> 00:04:04.639 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the city of Bolívar, the wooden cathedral of Georgetown, the wooden
Dutch houses of Paramaribo, the equator capital under a volcano, and the gold
of Bacatá.
